== Results ==

The first round of voting was held on 10 November 2019. Incumbent president [[Klaus Iohannis]], of the ruling [[National Liberal Party (Romania)|National Liberal Party]] (PNL) led the field with 37.8 percent, with former Prime Minister [[Viorica Dăncilă]], of the opposition [[Social Democratic Party (Romania)|Social Democratic Party]] (PSD) finishing second with 22.26 percent. Because no candidate obtained the support of more than 50% of registered voters,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.romania-insider.com/romania-presidential-elections-2019-partial-results-first-round|title=Romania presidential elections 2019: Partial results confirm that incumbent president and former PM move to second round - update|website=Romania Insider|language=en|access-date=2019-11-25|archive-date=2019-11-16|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191116010955/https://www.romania-insider.com/romania-presidential-elections-2019-partial-results-first-round|url-status=live}}</ref> the second round was held two weeks later, on 24 November 2019, between Iohannis and Dăncilă. Exit polls on election night showed Iohannis winning handily.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2019/11/voting-romania-presidential-runoff-191124090725551.html|title=Exit polls: Romania's President Iohannis wins re-election|date=24 November 2019|website=Al Jazeera|access-date=25 November 2019|archive-date=24 November 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191124231019/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2019/11/voting-romania-presidential-runoff-191124090725551.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.france24.com/en/20191124-pro-european-incumbent-wins-romania-presidential-elections|title=Pro-European incumbent wins Romania presidential elections|date=24 November 2019|website=France 24|language=en|access-date=25 November 2019|archive-date=28 November 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191128013647/https://www.france24.com/en/20191124-pro-european-incumbent-wins-romania-presidential-elections|url-status=live}}</ref> He ultimately finished with 66.09 percent of the vote, the second-highest vote share for a winning presidential candidate in direct, popular elections in Romania's democratic history since 1990 onwards.

Dan Barna openly endorsed Iohannis in the second round. Theodor Paleologu positioned himself as against Dăncilă, without openly endorsing Klaus Iohannis (his party, PMP, openly endorsed Klaus Iohannis). Kelemen Hunor openly endorsed Iohannis in the second round (his organization, UDMR/RMDSZ, position itself as non-partisan). Alexandru Cumpănașu positioned himself against Dăncilă, without openly endorsing Iohannis on the other hand however.

